### 3.4 CaGAT architecture

The overall layer-wise propagation of the proposed CaGAT is summarized in Algorithm 1, where σ​(⋅)𝜎⋅\sigma(\cdot) used in the last step denotes an activation function, such as ReLU​(⋅)=max⁡(0,⋅)ReLU⋅0⋅\mathrm{ReLU}(\cdot)=\max(0,\cdot). Considering the efficiency of CaGAT training, we employ a truncated iteration algorithm to optimize the context-aware problem approximately in CaGAT architecture. In this paper, we apply CaGAT on semi-supervised classification. Similar to many other works [[10](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1910.01736#bib.bib10), [17](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1910.01736#bib.bib17)], the optimal weight matrix W and weight vector ΘΘ\Theta of each hidden layer in CaGAT are trained by minimizing the cross-entropy loss via an Adam algorithm [[9](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1910.01736#bib.bib9)] which is initialized by using Glorot initialization [[6](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1910.01736#bib.bib6)]. Figure 1 shows the training loss values across different epochs. One can note that, CaGAT obtains obviously lower cross-entropy loss values than GAT at convergence, which clearly demonstrates the higher predictive accuracy of CaGAT model.

